course: dictionary
lesson: 2898
title: One word, two meanings: dough
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the clues in the sentence tell you which one the writer means. «They trap the gas, so the dough swells, and every hole in a slice of bread is where a bubble once was.» Bread, swells and bubble all point to baking, so dough here is the soft mixture that becomes bread.
explain: Look at the words near dough before you decide. Words like knead, roll out, yeast and bake point to cooking. Words like pay or spent would point to money. Read the whole sentence first, then try your meaning in it to check.
explain: Dough is a noun and usually has no plural, so you say «some dough» or «a lump of dough», not «two doughs». The second meaning, money, is informal and old slang, so you may find it in older stories, but it is not used in cooking.
fact: Dough is a thick, soft mixture of flour, water and other things.
fact: Dough is made before baking, for bread, pizza, pastry or biscuits.
fact: «Yeast is a tiny living fungus; in dough it eats sugar and gives off carbon dioxide gas that puffs the dough up.»
fact: Warmth makes yeast work faster, so dough is often left somewhere warm to rise.
fact: Dough can also mean money, but this is informal, old slang.
exercise: «They roll out the dough on the floured table before cutting out the biscuits.» What is the dough in this sentence? | A soft cushion for sitting on | A type of money | A small loaf of bread that is already baked | *A soft mixture of flour and water for baking
exercise: «Rosa had plenty of dough, so she bought the new bike.» What does dough mean here? | *Money | A kind of cake that has been baked | A soft mixture for making bread | A bag for carrying shopping
exercise: «The yeast gives off gas, so the dough rises.» Why does the dough rise? | It is made of sugar and water only | Someone pushes it flat with a rolling pin | *Gas from the yeast gets trapped inside it | It is put in the fridge to freeze
test:
Why is dough sometimes left somewhere warm? | *Warmth helps yeast work faster, so the dough rises | Warmth stops the dough from ever rising | Warmth turns the dough into money | Warmth makes the dough freeze solid
What does dough mean in «cookie dough»? | *A soft mixture for baking cookies | Money for buying cookies | A cookie that has already been baked | A tool for rolling out cookies
exam:
Which sentence uses dough to mean money? | She spent the morning kneading the dough | *He said he was short of dough until payday | The dough swelled up in the warm kitchen | They rolled the dough out thin on the table
